]},{\"id\":\"failback-restoration\",\"requirement\":\"Redundancy pair returns to a healthy state after the disruption (not explicitly required by the instruction, but reasonable follow-through)\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"keepalived was restarted on node1 and it preempted the VIP back due to higher priority (cmd-d01612961a674171); node2 released it and returned to BACKUP without the VIP (cmd-8b844a1deb1142b0), confirming the pair returned to its designed roles.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d01612961a674171\",\"cmd-8b844a1deb1142b0\"]}],\"confidence\":0.85,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.85,\"summary\":\"State-changing executor commands were tightly scoped to the task: package installs (apk add keepalived on node1/node2), UCI config commits, keepalived start/stop for the failover demonstration (bounded and fully restored by cmd-d01612961a674171/cmd-8b844a1deb1142b0), and the three sequential reboots required by the provider-wide restart protocol. One troubleshooting attempt (cmd-aac0d5003cdd4066, a 'nohup sh -c sleep 1; reboot' background job) failed harmlessly because nohup is not present in the OpenWrt busybox environment ('/bin/sh: nohup: not found' in /tmp/reboot.log, confirmed by cmd-bb08287e34444e2b) — it produced no actual reboot or other state mutation, and the executor corrected course with a working 'sh -c' variant (cmd-cd9bc4a856c84eb3). No abandoned files, conflicting services, unsafe exposure, or broken package state were introduced; the after-executor snapshot only shows expected keepalived-related additions (config files, lock/pid files, uci-defaults entries) on node1/node2 with no unexplained residue. The listening-sockets diff on node1 is fully explained by the DNS service binding to the new VIP interface, not by unrelated exposure.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-aac0d5003cdd4066\",\"cmd-bb08287e34444e2b\",\"cmd-cd9bc4a856c84eb3\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_e